plan React
title   React ownership, attachment and passive views
status  proposed
goal
    Components read consistent data and keep stable actor handles without starting work in render.
outcomes
    O1  Provider and Runtime ownership
        flow-state/react exposes <FlowProvider runtime={runtime}>{children}</FlowProvider>.
        It accepts an existing Runtime and children, never constructs or disposes the Runtime,
        and renders children immediately without suspending or showing a readiness fallback.
        Effects await shared ready(); unmount cancels only that observer, not shared readiness.
        Readiness failure reaches a React error boundary on the next render.
        Hooks use the nearest Provider; unrelated nested Runtimes never share data or ownership.
        useView rejects a foreign Runtime's handle with a provenance FlowError before subscribing.
        The host constructs a new Runtime for a principal switch and disposes the old one;
        changing the Provider key alone does not dispose it. Isolation follows @plan.Runtime.O7.
        All useView reads in one render see one consistent version across actors and store entries.
    O2  Hooks and the first render
        useActor(machine, options?) returns one fresh local actor handle per component incarnation.
        useActorByRef(ref, options) ensures a shared actor and returns a handle for a non-null ref;
        any options object, including {}, selects ensure. Declared input must have its exact type.
        useActorByRef(ref) only looks up and returns Actor | null; missing or disposed means null.
        A null ref returns null and starts nothing. Invalid, foreign, opaque-local or mismatched
        refs reject with a provenance FlowError. Lookup neither subscribes nor retains an actor.
        Before attachment, ensure returns a stable handle with a pure snapshot: default states,
        initial(input) memory, available passive provider context, active lifecycle, no issues,
        active resource lanes Pending with undefined data or a pure placeholder; inactive lanes 
        absent.
        Pending lanes alone do not mark optimistic writes pending. Attached actors show committed 
        data.
        Render registers nothing, subscribes to nothing, starts no work, writes no storage and emits
        no evidence, including lazy adoption. Pure initializers may run in discarded renders.
        Fresh attachment keeps the prepared memory; restored/shared actor precedence is 
        @plan.Runtime.O3.
        Server and hydration snapshots follow @plan.ServerRendering.O4.
        Handles, sends and typed event constructors stay stable for the same identity. Equivalent
        refs resolve the same actor; changing the ref resolves the new identity. Local input is read
        once; changing local machine or input requires a keyed remount and emits a development 
        diagnostic.
        Shared input never resets an existing actor; conflicting ref options follow 
        @plan.Runtime.O3.
    O3  Attach, detach, Strict Mode and grace
        After commit and readiness, an ensuring hook attaches the same handle through the Runtime.
        It uses current provider context without replaying initial, input or onContext work.
        Lazy admission follows @plan.LazyFeatures.O2; failed admission leaves no partial actor.
        Before attachment, at most 64 sends buffer in FIFO order; send 65 throws
        FlowError(AdmissionOverloaded) without accepting the event. Attachment delivers accepted 
        sends
        exactly once through @plan.Runtime.O4. Closed or abandoned handles reject later sends;
        discarded renders never deliver their buffered events to another component incarnation.
        Local cleanup leaves the actor active for 100 milliseconds on the Runtime Clock. Reattach
        before expiry keeps the actor and cancels disposal; expiry disposes it through the Runtime.
        Work continues during grace. Strict Mode cleanup/setup never duplicates work or loses the 
        actor.
        Shared ensuring hooks count as uses until cleanup; retention follows @plan.Runtime.O3.
        Dropping a JavaScript handle disposes nothing. Explicit disposal is visible to all users;
        a mounted ensuring hook re-ensures a fresh incarnation under @plan.Runtime.O6.
        Unmount while awaiting readiness never admits later. Reattach during disposal waits for
        cleanup; failed cleanup surfaces through @O5. Activity hiding beyond grace loses local state
        under the current policy; reveal creates a fresh actor, subject to @Q1.
    O4  Eligibility in views
        useView(actor, select) accepts an exact handle and preserves the selector's exact return 
        type.
        Nullable lookup must be branched first; Machines, refs and comparator arguments are 
        rejected.
        Selectors read frozen state, memory, context, issues, lifecycle and activities. A disposed
        snapshot keeps its last state, memory and context with lifecycle disposed.
        State tokens narrow snapshot types; check(s, event) follows @plan.Machines.O4.
        Activity data follows @plan.Operations.O6; inspection data follows @plan.Inspection.O1.
        descriptor.get and Entity.get are passive: reading never fetches, mutates or retains data.
        Selectors start no work and acquire no ownership. Transactions have no view reader.
        Changes to currently selected descriptor keys update the view even without an actor change;
        conditional reads replace those dependencies. Selected values compare with Equal.equals,
        and unchanged lanes keep identity. Changing selector identity does not resubscribe to the 
        actor.
    O5  Errors in views
        A selector exception reaches the nearest React error boundary. Reading unchanged relevant
        data rethrows the same exception without reevaluation; a later matching publication retries,
        while unrelated updates do not. An attached view failure leaves committed data and siblings 
        intact.
        Initial attachment or hydration validation failure aborts admission without a partial actor.
        Readiness, admission, lazy adoption, provenance and cleanup failures retain the FlowError
        classification of @plan.Diagnostics.O1 and rethrow on the next render. Boundary reset allows
        adoption retry on a later attachment, never by loading during render.
        Resource failure lanes and typed machine errors remain view data; they do not automatically
        throw. Unknown commit reconciliation follows @plan.Operations.O3. Hooks never suspend.
verification
    V1  Type/mount tests prove exact hook types, Provider isolation, readiness and boundary errors.
    V2  Render tests prove pure snapshots, stable handles, ref changes and no discarded-render work.
    V3  Attach tests prove 64 buffered sends, FIFO delivery once, current context and unmount 
         safety.
    V4  Lifecycle tests prove grace, Strict Mode, retention, re-ensure and cleanup failures.
    V5  View tests prove consistent passive reads, key updates, equality and isolated error retries.
    V6  Hydration tests prove matching first HTML and no duplicated work.
questions
    Q1  Should local actors survive React Activity hiding beyond grace?
        Current behavior expires them; shared refs offer explicit retention. Local retain is not a 
        v1 option.
    Q2  Deferred to build time
        Choose the private attachment and subscription implementation.
        Choose how selector failures are cached for unchanged relevant data.
        Choose development diagnostic wording for local machine/input changes.
        Choose lifecycle race generation and browser test coverage.
